---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/docs.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Scheduling and runs — Querial">
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
            <p class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-querial-sky">For DevOps</p>
            <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mt-1">Scheduling and runs</h1>
        </div>
        <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none space-y-6 [&>p]:text-muted-foreground [&>h2]:text-foreground">
            <p>
                Querial includes a first-party durable scheduler. You do not need OS cron
                for production. Friendly presets cover common cadences; advanced mode
                accepts full cron. Help for expressions opens in a markdown modal.
            </p>

            <h2>What the scheduler does</h2>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
                <li>Persists due work in the control database and recovers after process restart.</li>
                <li>Creates runs when a schedule fires. Versions with <code class="text-foreground">external-parquet</code> roots cannot be scheduled — attach files via <a href="/docs/devops-trigger" class="text-link hover:underline">Trigger API or Run now</a>.</li>
                <li>Can wait on upstream artifacts before planning downstream steps (artifact-aware scheduling).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The scheduler does <strong class="text-foreground">not</strong> execute SQL.
                Registered agents pull leases for step work. Until DAG and agent flags are
                on, legacy serial execution remains available.
            </p>

            <h2>Full-graph plans</h2>
            <p>
                Manual, scheduled, retry, and backfill runs always <strong class="text-foreground">plan</strong>
                the full published version graph. On those default paths every planned node
                is <strong class="text-foreground">executed</strong>. Cheap repeats are
                author-owned via idempotent SQL (MERGE, watermarks, version guards). Querial
                does not skip nodes that “look already done” for those triggers. Failed-cone
                recovery is a separate operator action —
                <a href="/docs/devops-retry" class="text-link hover:underline">Retry and failed-cone recovery</a>.
            </p>

            <h2>Concurrency</h2>
            <p>
                Pools, per-connection limits, and queue aging keep one noisy tenant from
                starving others. Control-plane timestamps are UTC; the Web UI shows the
                user's preferred timezone.
            </p>

            <h2>Run visualizer</h2>
            <p>
                The visualizer is the published graph with live status (including SSE).
                Root nodes are badged. Fan-in nodes with two or more inbound edges show
                compact ALL / ANY badges for the snapshotted join mode. Retry actions
                live on run details and the schedule run list, not on the visualizer
                canvas.
            </p>
            <p>
                Join mode is taken from the run snapshot, not from later draft edits.
                Wait any does not cancel losing branches. Fail-fast still skips a
                pending Wait any consumer before OR logic can start it. Skip reason
                <code class="text-foreground">wait_any_unsatisfied</code> means every
                inbound parent finished and none satisfied its edge. Design-time rules:
                <a href="/docs/developer-join-mode" class="text-link hover:underline">Wait all and Wait any</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</DocsLayout>
